 1 big thing — Exclusive: New Bibi, Trump rift 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Photos: Alessandra Benedetti/Corbis and Win McNamee/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Axios' Barak Ravid, who has closely covered President Trump's relationship with Bibi Netanyahu and Israel going back to the 2016 election , writes: 

 When Benjamin Netanyahu and Donald Trump met in the Oval Office two weeks ago, the president asked the Israeli prime minister how he was doing in the polls ahead of the Oct. 27 election.

 Netanyahu paused. Then one of his advisers piped up: "Mr. President, he is winning," the aide said, according to a U.S. official familiar with the exchange. Why it matters: Netanyahu isn't winning. And Bibi wants an endorsement from Trump, who so far hasn't given it, despite being asked about it repeatedly by reporters. That White House scene, first reported here, captures the tension. 

 The president could still change his mind. But with just 74 days left until Israel's election, time is running short for Netanyahu. 🖼️ The big picture: In the first few weeks of the war in Iran, some of Trump's close advisers were confident he would endorse Netanyahu.

 But as time passed and the war bogged down, the two men's foreign policy goals — and their domestic political interests — started to diverge. More and more daylight has opened between them over the last three months — not just on Iran, but on Lebanon and Gaza as well. Two senior U.S. officials said that while Trump likes Netanyahu on a personal level, he's been frustrated by his decisions and policies.

 "Bibi is his own worst enemy," Trump said, according to two people who spoke to the president about Netanyahu recently. President Trump and Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu meet in the Oval Office last month. Photo: Ma'ayan Toaf/Israeli Government Press Office via Getty Images Behind the scenes: Several Netanyahu political adversaries have expressed concern that Trump will endorse the incumbent leader, two of their aides told Axios.

 According to two sources with knowledge of the outreach, those opposition figures back-channeled messages to Trump and his aides through mutual friends, donors and other political allies, urging the president to remain neutral. A former U.S. official briefed on the situation claimed that Netanyahu's weakness in polls has deterred Trump from getting involved. The intrigue: Trump was asked by reporters four times in recent weeks whether he endorses Netanyahu. Each time, he has stopped short of doing so.

 In several recent interviews, Trump praised Netanyahu for being a "great wartime prime minister," but each time referred to him in the past tense. 👀 What to watch: The Israeli election is crucial for Trump's Middle East agenda for his final two years in office, especially with the Iran crisis in a "no deal, no war" limbo.

 Trump also wants to push forward his Gaza peace plan, make progress in negotiations between Israel and Lebanon, cinch a security agreement between Israel and Syria and, most importantly, broker a historic peace deal between Israel and Saudi Arabia. Share this story . 

 
 
