 3. 🌾 The new rural America 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Census Bureau, USDA. (Census data and BLS rural designations are released in different years.) Chart: Erin Davis/Axios Visuals The U.S. is projected to become majority-minority in 2046, but hundreds of rural pockets have already crossed that demographic threshold, an analysis by Axios' Russell Contreras and Erin Davis finds. 

 Why it matters: The evolving face of rural America, driven both by immigration and increased mobility, has invigorated communities, increased their diversity — and presented new challenges.

 The shifts are putting unprecedented pressure on rural schools, courts, health departments and election offices, which often are cash-strapped with limited personnel. 🧮 By the numbers: An Axios analysis of federal data finds that 403 U.S. counties — roughly 12.8% of the national total — already are majority-minority.

 That includes 221 "non-metro counties" and 182 counties in urban and suburban metro areas. State of play: Texas leads the nation with 57 rural majority-minority counties — all Hispanic along the Southwest border and into the oil-rich Panhandle. Mississippi has 25 such counties, followed by New Mexico with 18, Georgia with 17 and Alaska with 15.

